From 3caa3b0d67860ac9e3ba063e6cad5c8aeb8b92c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lane <lane@local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 01:47:33 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 25/29] test(oracles): fix the oracle test files' own stale
 closure/factory/revision literals

These twelve tests/test_*.py files each keep an independent copy of the
same provenance data the resync (bbb48dc, 5d99466) just corrected --
either as a hardcoded Python literal compared against the live oracle
JSON, or as a Python string embedded in a generated C++20 smoke-test
source that the test compiles with -fsyntax-only. Before this change every
one of them still asserted the stale 22-file authority closure, and a
few carried their own separately-stale value:

  - test_mandelbrot_oracle.py / test_newton_oracle.py: the old canonical
    factory name (canonicalFactory252 / canonicalFactory264) instead of
    the renumbered 260 / 272.
  - test_testpattern_oracle.py: the old testPattern-oracle-coherence.json
    anchor hash, plus a second, separate `== 22` on the closure length.
  - test_lightleak192_oracle.py: a mutation test that intentionally
    corrupts the authority to add one dynamic-import file and asserts the
    resulting rejection message names the pinned/observed cardinality --
    updated from "expected 22, found 23" to "expected 23, found 24".
  - test_fractal_oracle.py / test_dither_preparation.py: an even older
    stale upstream_revision, "117a236679d1db3ab8f0e278230ece277b57564c"
    (older than the a024dc3a corpus hash the rest of the campaign was
    keyed on), plus -- for dither -- the pre-remap canonical-kernels.js
    sha256 and byte count (1713290 -> 1747097, since canonical-kernels.js
    grew when the authority added the remapFactory) and the pre-remap
    23-entry inline closure fixture used by its own -Wall -Wextra -Werror
    C++ smoke test.

Also fixes a real, unrelated-to-any-of-the-above bug this run turned up:
eight generator/materializer .sha256 sidecars (median, distortion,
fractal, dither, texture x1, glitch x2, and
generate_parallax_native_oracle_include.py) were left recording a stale
digest after an editing pass that touched the target file after the
sidecar had already been refreshed. Every *.sha256 sidecar in the tree was
swept and reconciled against hashlib.sha256() of its target file's current
bytes; the eight mismatches were corrected in place (fixed in bbb48dc's
tree, since the affected files live under docs/port-engineering and
tools/glslcpp already staged there) and the sweep re-run clean.

No package's render_cases/mutation_ledger pixel data is touched by this
commit -- every change here is a test file asserting the same provenance
facts bbb48dc already established in the generators/materializers/.inc
files, or a test file's independent copy of a closure/revision fixture
brought back in sync with them.

Verified: the 16 affected test modules (the twelve above, plus
test_kaleido_oracle.py, test_median_preparation.py, test_texture_oracle.py,
and test_top_level_oracle_unittest_bridge.py, all of which needed no text
change but were re-run to confirm the sidecar fix alone cleared them) now
pass 161 passed, 4 skipped, 0 failed under the CI-style authority env.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01EU6jaFnvpzrcsgg8pXrwK2
